content:
Problem summary:
- Intermittent client disconnects and truncated SSE streams reported by some customers when our hosted API is under sustained load. Symptoms vary: client receives partial events, connection drops with 'net::ERR_INCOMPLETE_CHUNKED_ENCODING', or long stalls (no events for 10-30s) then a flurry of events.

Context / recent changes:
- Rolled out small change to response buffering in runtime (node/express layer) around 2025-02-05 to reduce CPU by batching flushes. Suspect this introduced or exacerbated backpressure interactions.
- Issues primarily on requests that stream many small events (avg 1-3 tokens per event) for > 30s.

Observed on:
- Chrome (fetch + EventSource polyfills), curl -N, Python aiohttp client (stream reading). Not consistent across all clients.
- Happens more when requests route through nginx LB + internal HTTP proxies.

Repro steps (commands I used locally):
1) Simple SSE server (node) to reproduce bad chunking pattern:
  # server.js (quick test)
  const http = require('http');
  http.createServer((req, res) => {
    res.writeHead(200, {
      'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream',
      'Cache-Control': 'no-cache',
      'Connection': 'keep-alive'
    });
    let i = 0;
    const t = setInterval(() => {
      i++;
      res.write(`data: msg-${i}\n\n`); // intentionally small writes
      if (i>200) { clearInterval(t); res.end(); }
    }, 50);
  }).listen(8088);

  # client (curl preserves streaming):
  curl -N http://localhost:8088/ > /tmp/out.txt

2) Compare behavior with and without manual flush/batching:
  - If I buffer 10 msgs and write them as one chunk, clients are stable. If I write many tiny chunks (as above) sometimes proxy/nginx collapses them leading to delayed delivery or truncation.

3) Test via HTTP proxy (nginx) to simulate production path:
  # minimal nginx conf snippet used locally:
  location /sse-test {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8088;
    proxy_buffering off;              # important for SSE
    proxy_cache off;
    proxy_set_header Connection "";
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    chunked_transfer_encoding on;
    # tried x-accel header too:
    proxy_set_header X-Accel-Buffering no;
  }

  curl -N --http1.1 http://localhost/sse-test > /tmp/out-proxy.txt

4) Client repro with Node fetch (simulate slow consumer/backpressure):
  # client.js
  const fetch = require('node-fetch');
  (async () => {
    const r = await fetch('http://localhost:8088');
    const reader = r.body.getReader();
    while(true) {
      const {done, value} = await reader.read();
      if(done) break;
      // simulate slow processing to create backpressure
      await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r, 120)); // 120ms per chunk
      console.log('got chunk', value && value.length);
    }
  })()

  - With slow consumer, server continues to write; sometimes connection stalls and client gets partial chunk then stream ends with an error.

Observed error snippets (from browsers / node / nginx logs):
- browser console: net::ERR_INCOMPLETE_CHUNKED_ENCODING
- node client: fetch failed: disconnect before complete body
- nginx error.log: upstream prematurely closed connection while reading response header from upstream
- runtime logs: write EPIPE on socket or client aborted request

Hypotheses / likely causes:
1) Proxy buffering + compression interaction: gzip transform can buffer until a minimum size -> small writes get coalesced/delayed. Setting 'proxy_buffering off' + 'gzip off' mitigates.
2) Our deliberate batching change (to reduce CPU) increases per-write atomicity; when combined with proxy buffering semantics, it changes chunk boundaries causing some clients to mis-handle streaming events.
3) Flow-control/backpressure not handled properly across layers: Node's socket buffer fills when consumer slow -> runtime may incorrectly assume data delivered and continue producing then get EPIPE.
4) Keep-alive vs connection header differences on proxies causing premature upstream close.
5) Intermittent TLS termination / LB idle timeouts closing connection during long-lived streams.

Quick fixes to try (local + prod canary):
- Ensure SSE responses have these headers: Content-Type: text/event-stream, Cache-Control: no-cache, Connection: keep-alive, Transfer-Encoding: chunked, X-Accel-Buffering: no (nginx).
- Disable gzip / chunked compression on LB for SSE routes (Cache-Control: no-transform or explicit route rule).
- In node runtime, prefer res.flush() after each event (when supported) to force immediate TCP write.
- Add periodic keepalive comment lines (":\n") every 15s to keep proxies/clients aware.
- Revert the batching change for SSE endpoints or add heuristics to disable batching for small-event streams.

Experiments run so far and results:
- Turning off nginx proxy_buffering locally removed the long stalls (events were delivered promptly).
- Disabling gzip for SSE endpoints reduced cases of truncated streams.
- Adding res.flush() reduced observed EPIPEs but did not eliminate them when consumer very slow.

Next action items / TODOs:
- [ ] Create a small canary feature flag to disable batching for streaming-response model endpoints and roll to 10% to check metrics (error rate, aborted streams). (Samir)
- [ ] Add automatic SSE headers and 'X-Accel-Buffering: no' in edge/proxy layer for known streaming routes. (Ava)
- [ ] Add more detailed socket-level logging around write failures and EPIPEs (include consumer RTT, socket buffer state if possible). (Jamie)
- [ ] Add an integration test that simulates slow consumers (reader sleeps) and asserts stream doesn't prematurely close. Make this part of CI for runtime. (Evelyn)
- [ ] Investigate LB idle timeout settings; consider keepalive comment ping frequency. (infra)

Notes / random findings:
- curl -N is the most reliable quick tester. Chrome sometimes hides errors behind fetch polyfills.
- httpie --stream is handy to visualize streaming output.
- Large customers hitting Dedicated paths had fewer reports -> suspects edge/proxy configuration differences.

Attachments / logs:
- /var/log/runtime/sse-2025-02-18.log (sample trace of EPIPE sequence)
- gist of curl outputs: https://gist.github.com/jamiet/xxxxx (internal)

If we want to pair on a repro tomorrow I can run a live test with our staging nginx + runtime and a slow client simulation.

Bottom line: likely a buffering/proxy + batching interaction. Short-term mitigation: enforce no-transform/no-buffering on streaming routes and revert batching for SSE. Longer-term: make runtime aware of streaming semantics (disable batching per-response) and add CI tests for slow consumers.
